In article <8bimtn$me9$1@slb6.atl.mindspring.net>, "Ryan Matteson"
<desc@mindspring.com> writes: 

> I am looking into getting a DEC Alpha for home use but cannot seem to
> find what the diff is between Multias, DEC Servers and the such so I do
> not know what to start looking for. Anyone have any vendors that sell used
> DEC Alphas and info on what models I should be looking at???

If you are lucky, you can get a free ALPHA with 192 MB memory, two SCSI
controllers and a huge colour monitor for free from this newsgroup.  :-)
I have been lucky.  :-)  :-)

The multia has only "unsupported support" for VMS.  Any old ALPHA will 
probably run VMS; some of the newer (but not extremely new) ones were NT 
only (as was a very old "ALPHA PC").

I would look for something with a name like DEC 3000 (don't you love the
way that numbers are re-used?  :-) ), ALPHAstation 200, ALPHAstation
255, ALPHAstation 500 etc.  A name like 250/4/233 refers to model, CPU 
generation and speed.  For home use, any generation or speed is likely 
to be enough.  Probably, you won't need a server (main difference: more 
I/O capacity; often SLOWER CPU than the corresponding workstation).




