Ryan Matteson <desc@mindspring.com> wrote:
> I am looking into getting a DEC Alpha for home use but cannot seem to
> find what the diff is between Multias, DEC Servers and the such so I do
> not know what to start looking for. Anyone have any vendors that sell used
> DEC Alphas and info on what models I should be looking at???

Well, you're leaving out one very important bit of information, namely what
OS you're planning on running on it.  Of course what use you plan on putting
it to makes a difference also.  Then there is the question of how much you
want to pay, and if this is for simply hobbyist use.

I'm going to assume you want to run a real OS on it, namely OpenVMS.  I'd
recommend against a Multia or one of the funky Windows NT only DEC Servers
that are out there now.  The DEC 3000's make a nice system for running
OpenVMS, and most are fairly inexpensive now.  The AlphaStation 200's are
also pretty good machines.  I've got a AlphaStation 500/333 and have to
recommend against it unless you can get one with a lot of memory, as the
memory is VERY expensive to upgrade.  On the mid-range, I really like the
looks of the PWS500au's.  On the high-end you could go for a brand-new DS10
or XP900.

Personally I recommend a couple cheap Alpha's, maybe a couple nice VAXen,
and one really nice Alpha all in a nice OpenVMS cluster.  Through in a
couple StorageWorks shelves full of disks and you've got a nice home
computer system :^)

			Zane





