Let me return to a question I posed a while back, but on which there 
never seemed to be agreement.

I want to mirror disks at physically separate locations, and NOT do it 
over the network.

Assume that only 1 disk in the mirror set is "active" at a time.

Assuming I have enough extra disks, the easiest way would be to do
disk-to-disk image backups, first from the source disk to a transport 
disk and then from the transport disk to the target disk.  Fine for all 
but the system disk; here, as opposed to booting from standalone backup 
or CD, one would probably boot of the transport disk, change the node 
name etc and then use the old system disk as the new transport disk etc.

Without enough extra disks and/or with just small changes on several 
disks, tape would probably be the way to go.

Will the following scheme work?

First, do an image backup of the source disk and restore this to the 
target disk.

When moving from the source to the target site, first do a
backup/since=backup of the source disk, and then restore this
/incremental on the target disk.  Keeping in mind that only one disk at 
a time is active, when moving from the new source (old target) to the 
new target (old source) disk, one does again a backup/since=backup and 
restores this /incremental on the new target (old source) disk.

Considering how the first target disk was created, I can't see why this 
won't work.

With the /incremental, it shouldn't just put new files on the target 
disk but also remove files which have since been deleted from the source 
disk, right?

Dropping the assumption that only one disk is active at a time, my guess 
is that the above will not work since on the /incremental restore, files 
which were created on the target disk but not on the source disk since 
the former was last backed up /since=backup (to update the old target or 
new source disk) will be deleted.  A non-incremental restore wouldn't 
have this problem, but neither would it delete files which should be 
deleted.

While I'm at it, why is there no mention in the documentation of a
disk-to-disk /image backup but without the /save_set qualifier? 

Can the target disk of an /image restore be a different size (assuming 
it's not to small) than the source disk?

Can one use the menu system on the OS CD for backing up the system disk,
installing, updating etc on VAX?  I've only done this on ALPHA; it's
much more convenient than standalone BACKUP. 


Phillip Helbig


