In article <IPVD4.360$75.8084@ptah.visi.com>, "Dave White" <spectrac@thebestisp.com> writes:
:I have been contacted by a local university that needs to get their data off
:of a VAXStation 2100 and onto a PC for backup to CD-R.  From my conversation
:with the user, it appears that the system is running some variant of Unix.
:I'm only familiar with using VMS on VAX'en, with Pathworks for PC transfer.
:Could anyone give any pointers to what I would need to get the files off
:their VAX via Ethernet?

  There exists a DECstation 2100, a VAXstation 3100, a VAXstation 2000, 
  an AlphaServer 2100 (itself fka the DIGITAL 2100 server), and a variety 
  of other systems with similar names.  But no VAXstation 2100 system...

  As was stated in another reply, the DECstation 2100 was typically 
  configured with the ULTRIX MIPS operating system.  Various VAX-based
  systems can use ULTRIX VAX and OpenVMS, and the Alpha-based systems
  have a variety of options.

  If you're stuck, have the folks read the model number off the sticker;
  off the sticker with the serial number and related...

  As for the mechanism most likely used for network file transfers, that 
  would probably be FTP.  

  Transfering files to PC systems for CD-R can potentially lead to various 
  formatting and access problems as UNIX, OpenVMS, and PC systems all have 
  rather different views of what comprises the file system.

