in article 0C6C4.1573$Og6.334515@tw12.nn.bcandid.com,
pwood@wilma.widomaker.com at pwood@wilma.widomaker.com on 3/22/00 11:34 AM
wrote something like:

> Hi,
> 
> I recently booted my 5000/120 and now nothing
> happens, well actualy the front light under the badge
> urns green, the fans spin up and the back LEDs go all
> read and stay there. Any idea how to fix this?

    Try resetting the NVRAM. There should be a single black jumper block
near the CPU module. Put a jumper on it and power up the system. The lights
should dance once it starts it self test. I believe the system will just sit
there if it can't find an available console device.

> I've taken out all the other sticks of ram in
> case it was a memory error, the external SCSI port is
> terminated (there is only an RRD-42 in the case),
> reseated the video card (PMAGB-B) as well as reseating
> the cpu card.

    The memory test is the last thing the PROM monitor, does before dropping
to >> or booting, SCSI is second last. RAM is probably not the problem.
    I assume that you do indeed have a monitor connected and are not trying
to use a serial console with a frame buffer installed, since the system will
default to g.c. instead of serial console unless you have setenv console s.

> One other odd thing is that when the machine
> is powered on and the cdrom is attached to the SCSI
> cain it does not power up. Removing the drive from the
> chain allows it to power up and eject the caddy.

    This is most likely because the SCSI controller is holding the bus
hostage in a reset state. I would try removing the terminator from the back
of the box, if it still freezes, try testing the CDROM in another computer
to verify that it isn't the root of the problem. Then check termination
termination power on the CDROM and HD themselves.

    Chris




