Some drives do a periodic recalibration to compensate for temperature changes
in the environment.  The RZ29B may be one of them.

As for why the drive disappears when you put the TLZ06-VA in the shelf...
It may simply be due to the age of the TLZ06-VA.  On some older devices, made
before wide SCSI became popular, the upper 8 bits of the SCSI data bus were
grounded, essentially inhibiting access to the bus by wide devices.  As wide
SCSI has become more popular, this practice of grounding the upper 8 bits was
stopped.  

What to do about it?  Well, I personally believe that tapes and disks should
not be mixed on the same bus.  So, one option is to put the tape drive on
another bus, if available.   Another option for the truly adventurous would be
to open the TLZ06-VA, locate the upper 8 bits on the internal flex cable, and
disconnect them by carefully cutting the traces on the flex.  No guarantees
that this will even work, even if done correctly, however.

        Stu



