: putting in an RZ28A-VA in the shelf, an unsupported combination, does....

Looking at the Systems and Options catalog of November, 1996, it says that the
TLZ06-VA must be at rev D06 and built after 28-Feb-1995 to work in a 16-bit
shelf.  

There is no RZ28A-VA.  However, the RZ28-VA requires rev C01 after Nov-1993,
and the RZ28B-VA is 16-bit compliant for all revs.

: > What to do about it?  Well, I personally believe that tapes and disks should
: > not be mixed on the same bus.  
: 
: Why not?  Mid-range plans are to run the BA356 in dual-bus mode anyway, 
: so I could put the tapes on the other bus, but I'm not there yet.

I've found that in some cases, particularly with simultaneously heavy tape and
heavy disk I/O, that problems occur.  An example is that if you have a large
number of I/Os queued to the tape drive, and a large number of I/Os queued to
the disk drive(s) (as in backing up disk to tape).  Let's say that the tape
drive hogs the bus for a little too long, causing one of the disk I/Os to
timeout.  The usual response of the device driver is to reset the SCSI bus.
Tape drives typically rewind to BOT upon a SCSI bus reset.  There goes your
backup...

: > So, one option is to put the tape drive on
: > another bus, if available.   Another option for the truly adventurous would be
: > to open the TLZ06-VA, locate the upper 8 bits on the internal flex cable, and
: > disconnect them by carefully cutting the traces on the flex.  No guarantees
: > that this will even work, even if done correctly, however.
: 
: I have the 8-bit personality module installed, not the 16-bit one.  Is 
: this correct?  

You insert the appropriate personality module according to the SCSI controller
you're connecting to.  

Again, the problem is likely to be that the TLZ06-VA is below the required
revision to run in a 16-bit shelf.

        Stu



