Phillip Helbig wrote:
> 
> I'm looking for a good description of the interrelationships between the
> following things regarding DAT tapes:

I don't know how helpful it is, but here is info on a couple of points:
 
>    o  SCSI1, SCSI2, SCSI3, narrow, wide, fast, slow

SCSI-1, -2, -3 refer to revision of the SCSI standard.  SCSI-1 is not
particularly standard.  It basically refers to anything before SCSI-2.

Most equipment out there is SCSI-2, with some SCSI-3 stuff starting to
show up.  In general, the standards are upwards compatible.  If
everything is properly connected physically, you can ususally mix
different SCSI levels on the same bus.

narrow/wide/fast/slow refer to hardware connections.  Narrow is 8 bit
and wide is 16 bit.  

Slow/Fast refer to the bus handshake protocols.  Slow is also called
asynchronous and fast is synchronous. Slow can support bus transfers at
up to 5M/sec and fast at up to 10M/sec.  Note that these rates are not
guaranteed.  Quite a bit of the async hardware maxes out at 1MB/sec.

Combining these, fast/wide has a theoretical maximum transfer rate of
20MB/sec. (10M transfers/sec and 2 bytes/transfer)

As I understand it, there is also Ultra (20MB/sec) and Ultra2
(40MB/sec).

Again, these protocols are upwards compatible.  If you put a slow device
on a fast bus, it will work, but transfers to/from that particular
device will be slow.  Transfer to/from other devices can be fast if the
devices support it.

While faster is generally better, faster also requires shorter cables. 
If you are only using one or two devices at a time, it may actually be
better to run the bus at a slower rate, especially if you need a longer
cable.

This is generally fine for workstations.

Where the faster protocols really shine is servers and disk farms.  Even
though a single device generally can not transfer at 80MB/sec
(Ultra2/wide), such a speed allows the device to squirt data from its
buffers and get off the bus.  This allows multiple devices to timeshare
the bus and transfer data "simultaneously".


>    o  DDS1, DDS2 etc
>    o  length of the tape in meters
>    o  capacity of the tape in GB and what this really means

DDS tapes are measured in meters.  DDS-1 (or just DDS) tapes are 60m
(1.3GB) or 90m (2GB).  Some early DDS tape drives can only be used with
60m tapes.

DDS2 tapes are 120m and store 4GB.

These capacities are raw, uncompressed numbers.  Compression is
generally advertised as doubling the storage, but the actual compression
varies depending on the data.

Compression in the PC world may be either through software or hardware. 
AFAIK, compression in the VMS world is always hardware.

A DDS tape drive may or may not have hardware compression.  If it does,
it is called a DDS-DC drive.  A DDS2 drive usually has hardware
compression.  Although I do have one that doesn't.  Until I saw that
drive (a Sony SDT-5200), I thought that all DDS2 drives had DC
compression.

