Before I continue with the rest of the reply, I should warn you that
what I'm describing is very likely not to work and might lose all the
data from your disk. USE AT YOUR OWN RISK!!

You could try using scu. Something like

scu -f /dev/rrz?c     (replace the ? by the correct digit)
scu> verify media starting 0

This is a non-destructive check of the disk. If you then want to take
the risk, you can try

scu> reassign lba xxxxxx      (where xxxxxx is the bad block number)

which will replace the bad block. Note that this is likely to corrupt
your file system, so you may find you have to "newfs" the bad partition
and hence lose all the existing data. Also from experience, the 
success rate is low. Having said that, I managed to reassign ~50 blocks
on a disk that clearly had a bad region (the blocks were adjacent) and
the disk has worked fine since then.

Regards,
John Hill

