In article <38dff5c6_1@127.0.0.1>, "Andrew" <arousch@home.com> writes:
>The DECNIS 500 has a part# DNSBE-BC.

The DECnis 500 is the 4 slot variant.
The DECnis 600 is the 9 slot variant.

Each have one processor (standard or high performance) and one memory card
(2MB or 8MB). Various line cards are also available.

One has been able to buy a DECnis package (and your part number above is
such a package) or buy the DECnis in pieces (because there is no package
consisting of exactly the cards you want) or buy a DECnis package and some
single pieces (because this is probably cheaper than buying all cards in
single pieces).
So, the number of the DECnis package does not neccessarily exactly
identify the DECnis router you have in front of you.

>                                     If there is no console cable how do
>you configure this thing?

At the time, the router was new or current, the usual method was the DECnis
Software package for an DEC Opsys (OpenVMS Alpha, OpenVMS VAX, Digital UNIX)
with DECnet/OSI installed. The DECnis software was available via the usual
software distribution channels (eg. the Consolidated CDROM distribution).

Later a Win32 product was also released. It was called the ClearVISN
DECnis Configurator (currently V3) and is still available at:

	http://www.networks.digital.com/dr/cdc/

You will also find some general DECnis docu there.

Current software for the DECnis (downloaded via net and eventually stored
in the DECnis processor card flash memory) is V4.1-7 (or maybe already V4.1-8),
but I bet, the version included in the CDC V3 is a little bit older...

