Stephen,

If the DEC part number on the main logic board is NOT 54-23606, I
don't think that the the system is capable of SMP operation.  The
BIOSes for the older (lower) part numbers of main logic board do not
recognize a second processor when it is plugged in.  All of the main
logic boards used in DECpc XL and Celebris XL systems look very
similar.  All use the Intel NX chipset.  But DEC made some changes in
the design of these boards.  

The latest DECpc/Celebris processor card has part number 54-24490.
Same story with the processor cards.  They all look just about the
same.  Only 54-24490 is designed to support CPUs faster than 133MHz,
because it has jumpers to set the higher clock multipliers required by
the faster CPUs.

I have not tried an Evergreen or other kit based on the AMD K6
processor.  Your guess is as good as mine.  The Celebris XL does work
with a 233MHz Pentium MMX, single processor.  There are several good
quality kits on the market, and you can make up your own with a
PowerLeap adapter and a Pentium MMX.  I do not know how any of these
faster processors will work on any board older than 54-24490.  The
BIOS will not correctly report the speed of any CPU faster than
200MHz, but this is only cosmetic, and typical of BIOS writers'
shortsightedness.

Replacing the main logic board is probably an hour of careful work,
less if you often work on the guts of personal computers.

... Ben Myers

