Yes, there should be tons of files. Also, I would guess that you have MicroVMS
and not VMS installed because of the lack of a system root directory such as
[SYS0.SYSEXE]. 

The owner probably deleted all deletable files from the system disk.

> of tapes with VMS on them, labeled as: VMS 5.4 BIN - warranty
> mandatory update and VMS 5.0-1 backup, complete system backup. Not
> sure if they contain any data.
> Any information that anyone can provide will be greatly appreciated!

The mandatory update probably won't install on what is left on your disk. You
probably don't have files such as VMSINSTAL.COM, etc required to update a
system. 

You'll need to find a tape with a standalone backup. Once you've booted
standalone backup, you'll have a very minimal VMS in memory with just the
backup command. You can then backup that 5.0 "complete system backup".

It won't be easy because you do not know what the name of the file (save set)
is on that tape.

normally, you'd issue a command such as:
	BACKUP/IMAGE/LOG/INIT MUA0:saveset.sav/SAVE  DUA0:

if the tape was an actual VMS tape, the save set you need would be VAXVMS050.B
from that tape.
After you used standalone backup to put the "B" saveset to your disk drive,
you can then boot and the rest of the installation procedure is automatically started.

Your best bet is to get the VMS hobbyst licence and try to get a kit on the
type of tape on your machine (TK50 ?). I am not sure you can install all of
VMS ona RD54, but there are options when you install to tailor some elements off.



