In article <8bdiui$r2d$1@info.service.rug.nl>, helbig@astro.rug.nl wrote:
>
>> Watch the memory in the VS4000-90: it goes in a non-obvious order, read the 
>> legend carefully! Get it wrong and you'll be scratching your head for a
> while.
>
>Can you elaborate?

If it was working before and all you have done is move it, then the memory is 
likely to be in the right order (although it's worth checking that it has not 
been jolted loose perhaps).

If you have four simms then from front to back you should have:
                simm
                empty
                simm
                empty
                empty
                simm
                empty
                simm

It's nicely symmetrical so it doesn't matter which end you start at in reading 
the above! However, since most other machines either bunch simms in groups of 
four or interleave them (which the above does not quite do) it is easy to see 
0D on the MLB for the first bank, 1A for the second and decide that the order 
is in-out-in-out-in-out-in-out. Then you sit there wondering why your machine 
doesn't want to play today.

OK so it's obvious and only a fool would get it wrong. I most certainly didn't 
waste half an hour a few months ago. No. Absolutely not :-)

Antonio

