No that's fine. The [SYSEXE] directory, as opposed to [SYSn.SYSEXE]
usually only has SYSBOOT.EXE in it. Something to do with early versions
of the primary bootstrap being only able to look up files in the MFD. On
something as modern as a MicroVAX II ;) it shouldn't be a problem.

PAGRDERR, OTOH, is worrying. It's often a hard disk fault ( or should
that be a hard hard disk fault?) when trying to hard fault an image into
memory. Maybe the RD54 isn't as new as you thought.






