In article <8b5o8v$l9q$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, unixb0y@my-deja.com writes:
>I have a DECserver 90M. When the ethernet backplane is not installed, i
>can use the RJ45 ethernet port. When i install the backplane, the BNC
>coax connector is enabled. How do i enable the RJ45 port when the
>backplane is enabled and disable the coax?

What do you mean by the "ethernet backplane" ?
Do you mean the DEChub90 ?

When the DECserver 90M is in the DEChub90, the RJ45 and the BNC port is
disabled, only the backpanel port (which is electrically a ThinWire Koax)
is enabled.

When the DECserver 90M is in standalone mode (with an external power supply),
both the RJ45 and the BNC is enabled but only one can be used.

When the DECserver 90M is in a stackable module (DETXX) but not stacked,
both the RJ45 and the BNC in enabled but only one can be used.
And the Position Identifier Dial mustn't be switched to the agent or bridge
position (IIRC).

When the DECserver 90M is in a stackable module (DETXX) and stacked,
the BNC in in use for the stack and the RJ45 is unusable (it is not a
repeater ;-).

