Kevin,

If the processor card in your system is NOT DEC part number 54-24490,
then you are limited to 133MHz.  If it is 54-24490, the max for which
the system was designed is dual 166s.  I've been told by once-DEC
hardware engineers, that a single P200 is OK, but not duals.  I have
also sold people Pentium MMX 233 upgrades using a PowerLeap adapter,
but I'm out of PowerLeap adapters right now.  Also, to upgrade to
either 166's or a 200, you need DEC part number 54-24002, which is a
3.52v voltage regulator needed for the faster Intel CPUs.  I may have
one kicking around here still.  The 54-24002 part is SCARCE.  The P166
and P200 are possible even tho the processor card sockets are labeled
Socket 5.

If the processor card is earlier than 54-24490 (lower part number),
its jumpers only allow a max of 133MHz.  If you or anyone else needs
the 54-24490 processor card for an upgrade, we have them in stock. 

Two other caveats... The Prioris XL (and Celebris XL) processor card
is designed so that little teeny tiny electronic components get in the
way of attaching a heat sink to the processor.  For this reason, DEC
used CPUs with permanently attached (epoxyed?) large heat sinks.

Second caveat... You need matched steppings for the CPUs.

If I still have a 54-24002 here (I still doubt it), I'll give you a
price on a pair of DEC P166s with heat sink + 54-24002 part.

In the meantime, the price on a DEC brand P133 with heat sink is $27 +
exact shipping.  Guaranteed to match the stepping on the P133
currently in your system, 'cause DEC bought a monster batch of these
CPUs and kept them in inventory for upgrades until recently when
Compaq sold them off.

Finally, there are no other processor card upgrade options for the
Prioris XL 5xxx.  The Prioris XL 5xxx used a case that appears to be
common with other small Prioris boxes, but on closer inspection one
would see differences in power supply and main logic board/processor
card layout... Ben Myers

