>If your Adaptec has 'Ctrl-A for SCSISelect' firmware at power up, you can
>configure the controller to issue a spin up command. IIRC, if it's a 1540/1542,
>it needs to be revision B or later, while all 2940 series have this facility.
>Also, it's possible that this message is suppressed, type Ctrl-A after the
>initial Adaptec message, but before it says bios loaded.

	Success.  The ST3106N jumper settings I found on the Web tallied
with reality; the RZ25L-E ones (from a German site) weren't even physically
correct.  I had to remove Jumper 6 from a block on the right side to get
the motor to spin.  Then I had a lot of toing-and-froing trying to get DOS to
accept it.  It wasn't until I had set up a DOS partition (no response),
low-level format[1] and DOS partition (again no response) that I tried to
look at the partitions on my Maxtor and received a message that triggered
a memory.  The Host Controller only works for SCSI IDs 0 and 1!

	Changed the jumpers on the left side for ID 1 instead of 6 and,
voila, a 510 MB drive D:, and all my disk IDs pushed up one (2 partitions
on the Maxtor, a RAM drive, and the Pioneer 6-disk CD-ROM changer).  Had to
do some fiddling about with swap drive settings (I'd had it on D:) to get
Windows 3.1 going, and then fiddle with default directory settings to get
my installed CD programmes going.  Encarta '95 was the only real problem --
it still wanted to access G: even after a reinstall, so finally I had to
uninstall and then install to get it finally to work on H:.

