Alex van Denzel wrote:
> 
> Bill wrote:
> 
> > I am using Hyperterm on a WinNT pentium box to try to talk to the '11.
> > I have tried every conceivable baud rate, leaving no parity and 1 stop
> > bit alone (we ALWAYS used this setup).  I get no response from any port,
> > including the SLU.
> 
> You have to set flow control to software (Xon/Xoff)

Correct.

> > My cable is as follows (yes, I rang it out with a meter):
> >
> > 9-pin pc end        pdp end
> > --------------        ---------
> > 1                        8
> > 2                        3
> > 3                        2
> > 4                        20
> > 5                        7
> > 6                        6
> > 7                        4
> > 8                        5
> > 9                        22
> 
> Too many wires connected. PDP-11's only use the send, receive and ground
> wires for terminal connections.

Not correct. PDP-11s don't use modem signals for flow control, but
you do need the correct signals asserted if you specify the terminal
line as a remote terminal.

For local terminals, the OS normally ignore everything but Tx and Rx.

I would recommend that you always use a cable with all the wires in place,
unless you know what you are doing. It never hurts to have all the wires
in place, but it *might* hurt if you don't...

> > If I plug a modem into the 25-pin end of the cable, I get verbal
> > responses to AT commands sent from Hyperterm, so I know I'm sending and
> > receiving from the pc via the cable.
> 
> Hmm. The PDP-11 has a male DB25 connector, just like the PC. Your modem
> should have a female DB25 connector. How you did fit the same cable in
> both connectors beats me :-)

Many vendors seem to not be aware of the fact that gender of the
connector is defined, and use whatever they seem to have in store.

This is one of the really sad things about RS-232, since life would
have been really easy if companies would have sticked to the standard.
As far as I know, only DEC and HP made an effort here.

> To summarize:
> 
> You need a null-modem cable, 9-pins female on one side, 25-pins female
> on the
> other side, connect pin 2 to pin 2, pin 3 to pin 3, and pin 5 (of 9) to
> pin 7
> (of 25). Combined with the software flowcontrol this should work.

Should work, unless the machine really wants all the signals...

	Johnny

