I've always found hyperterm to be a bit iffy. Appears to latch up under certain
conditions of modem control line input, especially if you are hacking around
connecting and disconnecting stuff while hyperterm is running. YMMV, but i've
always found kermit to be a much better bet as a general purpose terminal
emulator and the X-on/Xoff flow control does actually work.

If you use kermit, you cross over Rx and Tx data (pins 2 and 3) on the lead and
connect pin 7 to pin 1 (signal ground / frame ground) and run that through end
to end as common. If you really want to make sure, jumper pins 5,6.8 and 20 (25
way D RS232 connector) at the PC end. You will need to translate these pinouts
for a 9 pin rs232 pc connector.

The only other thing is baud rate, which should be selectable at the pdp end.
You want 8 bits, no parity, 1 stop bit at the terminal emulator end.

Never failed to work for me.

