: as well (not even remotely) as an Alpha at a comparable price. 

Would have been nice - if it were true. Unfortunately, it is not: alphas
do run scientific applications *very* well indeed (heck, we do use a few,
too), but, most of the time, they cannot compete with more high-volume
systems on price/performance. Just as an illustration, check out how many
beowulf-style clusters, which are primarily motivated by price/performance,
are built around alphas, and how many use x86 hardware...

Certainly, there are some types of workloads where alphas win on price/
performance, too - but most of the time, you'll pay a premium for the
superior absolute performance. As the market says you should, really.

/Serge.P

