I tend to think that one can't ignore Microsoft (much as one might wish to),
and that interface-level CIFS compatibility (or superset-compatibility),
possibly via a thin facade, is likely a requirement for a file system such
as I described (for that matter, it should likely be close to
NTFS-superset-compatible as well, since as I remember CIFS is a subset of
NTFS).

But the only existing CIFS-compatible distributed file system facilities I'm
aware of are client/server in nature rather than shared-disk, and the latter
offers the performance, availability, and scalability superiority I
mentioned - by taking advantage of the SAN environment in ways that
client/server architectures do not.

- bill

