Serguei Patchkovskii wrote:
> 
> Dirk Munk (munk@home.nl) wrote:
> : the data fits into cache it will run much faster. For the PWS 500 series
> : this also applies to benchmark results. With 4MB cache the specfp95
> : results are much better than with 1MB cache, most likely because the
> : benchmark program(s) fit better into cache.
> 
> Since, according to the official results on the www.specbench.org, neither
> Digital nor Compaq had posted any SPECcpu95 results for DPW/PWS-series 500
> model with either 1 nor 4 Mbytes of L3 cache (all the DPW 500 results are
> for 2Mb L3 model), would you mind to explain exactly what are you talking
> about?

At the time I bought my system it was on the Digital website, and it was
the reason why I paid a few extra $$$ for the 4 MB module instead of
going for the the 2 MB version. You can find a few figures at 
http://www.compaq.com/products/workstations/digital/auseries/techspecs.html
but with no specification of the amount of L3 cache used for the test.
However it seems we are talking about different benchmarks here, because
the table on this link refers to SPECfp_rate_base95 not specfp95. But a
difference of 10 to 15% between 2MB cache and 4MB cache is about right I
think. This may not look like very much until you compare the figures of
the 433, the 500 and the 600 models and see how little they differ.  

> 
> The difference between SPECfp95 results for AlphaServers based on 21164
> running at the same clock frequency as in DPW 500 au (500MHz), but with
> different amounts of L3 are certainly noticeable - but hardly staggering:
> 
> System                                       peak/base
> ------                                       ---------
> AlphaServer 1000A 5/500, 8Mb L3:             17.8/16.8
> AlphaServer  800 5/500, 2Mb L3:              16.1/15.0
>
True, but if you see what people pay for 33 or 50 MHz more on a Intel
processor, and the performance gain they get... The point is that more
L3 cache will give you a noticable extra performance, but normally not
100% more. And for the Seti program it is a known fact that a lot of
cache will speed things up quite a bit.


 
> Even the difference between having *no* L3 at all, and having 2Mb of L3 is
> fairly marginal for SPECfp95, if you ask me - albeit at a substantially
> lower clock rate:
> 
> AlphaServer 4100 5/300, 21164-300, 2Mb L3:   12.7/11.6
> AlphaServer 4100 5/300E, 21164-300, no L3:   12.0/10.9
> 
> /Serge.P
