    Is this a published policy somewhere, or just up to the discrection
of the local service manager? Over the years there's been lots of
assertions about this sort of thing regarding 3rd party gear in a
DEC system, but I've never found it to be an issue in practice. I wonder
what they do if the home built device uses an empty canister purchased
directly from Digital (they used to sell these and I'm sure there's still
lots of them around ). How on earth can they justify this if the
"home built" unit contains the same drive they sell ( eg Seagate ). Granted
the firmware might be slightly different but I don't see how that could damage
the system or the enclosure ( "damage", as opposed to "doesn't work right
if the 3rd party widget is present" ).

  What do they do if the system/subsystem is under a maintenance
agreement - will they refund all money paid on the agreement up to the
point they declare it void?

   How could any rational company think such an attitude is good for
business?

   OTOH, I recall one of Digitals excuses for stopping the sale of empty
canisters was that customers might try putting an IDE drive in one and
thus damage the subsystem. I find it hard to believe anyone could be that
stupid, but certainly I wouldn't expect any company to honour a warranty
on a system under those circumtances. If they can't prove "beyond a
reasonable doubt" that the 3rd party gear caused the damage then any
decent company would still honour the warranty.
 




