> DEC system, but I've never found it to be an issue in practice.

I don't know if there's a similar law in Canada, but here in the
US there's the "Magnusson-Moss Act" which says a lot about third
party equipment, service, and warranties.  IANAL but - very
briefly - the installation of non-DEC parts or non-DEC service
on a DEC system will not automatically void the warranty.  If the
parts or service cause damage to the system, that's a different
matter.

The Magnusson-Moss Act is most commonly applied to automobiles,
and is what allows you to do oil changes and other maintenance
yourself without "voiding the warranty".  Consumer protection law
in Europe and Asia is substantially different; as I understand
it in some places you're required *by law* to have normal maintenance
like oil changes and brake work done at a manufacturer's licensed
facility, and you can be fined or put into jail for attempting to do
the work yourself or by a non-manufacturer-approved mechanic.

Tim.



