In article <0304c290.09045cb6@usw-ex0107-050.remarq.com>, DR7 <drsevenNOdrSPAM@hotmail.com.invalid> writes:
>The drive is a digital model SZ03B, which has RZ24L-E on the Quantum HD
>inside the case. Quantum tells me "According to the serial number the
>drive is an OEM for Digital Equipment Corp. so I doubt if it will work
>in your system. When an OEM buys a drive from Quantum they will make
>changes to the drive that we are not privy to. They can make changes
>the firmware so that the drive will only work in their (the OEM's)
>system." Is this true? The drive seems to spin up well, but is there
>something about this unit that makes it impossible for me to reformat?
>If so, does anyone out there want to buy this puppy?

What you were told was not quite correct.

Digital (now Compaq) did and does buy it's SCSI drives from other
vendors.  They are then tested under much more strenuous conditions
than most PC vendors.  Usually, changes had to be made to the
firmware to make the drives run really reliably.  I believe, but
don't know for certain, if those changes were made generally
available to the manufacturer.

In any event, there is nothing to stop you from formatting
the drive.  I have in fact used a variety of Digital re-branded
SCSI drives in various Macintoshes, specifically including the
RZ24L-E which is currently the 'scratch' disk in my G3.

The problem that you are probably having is that for a long time
the drive setup program which came with the MacOS only recognized
drives which contained firmware specifically designed for Apple.
If you are running an old version of MacOS (7.anything), you will
need a third-party driver to format ANY drive that wasn't purchased
directly from Apple.  (Or, in some cases, you can 'hack' the software
to format non-Apple drives).

If you are running MacOS 8.1 or newer, it should recognize and format
the drive.  As I said, I have personally done this several times.
If you are running the current software and drive setup won't format
the drive, either you haven't plugged it in properly, or you have
a unit number conflict, or the drive is bad.  Failure to format
has nothing to do with the fact that it had the (improved) Digital
firmware installed in it.

-- 
