Terry C. Shannon (shannon@world.std.com) wrote:

: "Jim Agnew" <agnew@hsc.vcu.edu> wrote in message
: news:38C46033.C83D6026@hsc.vcu.edu...
: > seti takes 40 hours on my p6/100 box if i turn the video off to a blank
: > screen.. with it, i get 200 hrs... may not that be it?

: The SETI documentation indicates that work units will take longer if you run
: the video. My system is set up to run the SETI display as a screen saver for
: perhaps a minute, then the screen goes blank. Hence my results--which vary
: significantly depending on the specific work unit being processed--probably
: are not affected much by the display.

The SETI@HOME graphics client is multithreaded, basically 1 graphics
thread, and 1 computation thread.  My dual Celeron shows 100% CPU utilization
if the graphics are displayed.  When I drop the program to the background
and no longer display the graphics, the utilization drops to 50%.

In this manner, when you only have 1 CPU, the display and the computation
ends up sharing the same CPU. 

: Incidentally, the SETI statistics are quite interesting... DELL is now in
: the Number Eight slot with an average CPU time of 3 hrs 21 mins per work
: unit. DELL must be running some way fast IA-32s to get results like that!

As I under stand it, SETI@HOME works on a series of FFT's from 2, 4, 8, ....
to very_big_power_of_2 dataset.  What happens is that at times, the data
is completely cache bound, then the working set overflows the cache, and
spills out into main memory.  So you're mostly bound by the slowest part of
that.  In that respect, for SETI@HOME, the more cache the merrier, up to 
about 1 or 2 MB or so.  The Xeon's are probably the fastest with the full
speed 2 MB L2.  I've ran SETI@HOME on a 400 MHz Pentium II Xeon with 1 MB
L2, and that took about 5 1/2 hours.  Scale that to a 550 MHz Xeon with 
2 MB L2, and 3 1/2 hours seems very reasonable.

--
SETI@HOME Hall of Shame Project  
80386DX33, 128KB cache.  IIT 80387 MathCo. 32 MB. Win95.
