Every few months there is a topic on Alpha future.    

Doesn't it seem that Alpha is in trouble?  A few years back Alpha was a
non-standard, but had the best performance - some people held out hope that
technical merit could help Alpha gain market share.   Now Alpha is still a non
standard, and it seems that its lead has decreased or even that it has been
passed.

Clock speed is not the only indication of performance, but it is one factor and
does make good press.   Remember when Alphas led the way?   Now they've been
stuck in the 600 mhz range for a long time and have been passed by Intel at 850
and the formally lowly AMD.   Intel is supposed to have a gigahtz box out soon
in production.

Remember when Alpha was the only 64 bit machine?  Now other manufacturers have
64 big machines.   Even bumbling Intel will get a real 64 bit machine some time
this millenium.

Being an NT machine used to be a ray of hope for some, but now this is a thing
of the past.   

Is there any hope for Alpha?

Andrew



