In article <89pa85$36m$1@server05.icaen.uiowa.edu>,
Doug Siebert <douglas-siebert@uiowa.edu> wrote:
>
>An expensive out-there heatsink might cost $1000 due to low economies of
>scale in making only a few, but that hardly begins to account for the
>price discrepancy.  Heck, they could probably get pretty similar pricing
>for a volume solution of something exotic like Kryotech, and at least
>get some performance boost for the extra cost :)

I doubt it.  Have you looked at the technology?  HP9000/800 has never
really been a "volume" architecture, and I'd bet it cost a lot to design
the cooling even in a K box, let alone anything newer.  The fabrication
costs alone for those liquid-to-air transfer thingies must be pretty
high. Not to mention the R&D.  Keep in mind that this is a passive,
reliable technology.  Liquid whatever circulating by convection through
pipes to a radiator.  VERY expensive to fabricate, I would expect.  Lots
of welds, and captive coolant fluid.  I'm not a mechanical engineer,
but I'd bet the fabrication cost alone of one of those K580 heatsinks
probably drastically exceeds the production cost of the CPU it protects,
let alone the cooling R&D.

I'd bet a nice Peltier device would be far cheaper.

By contrast, the TurboCooler is little more than a fancy helical heatsink
with a fan inside.  Cheap, cheap, cheap.

Maybe they could put TurboCoolers in servers, but then they would suffer
CPU meltdown with a single fan failure, not to mention having very poor
density.  The TurboCooler for a 400MHz PA-8500 is several inches tall.

Now, I apologize if you can demonstrate awareness of the above issues
and you still maintain that a K580 or newer server CPU should cost
less than $1000 to fabricate the package.  On the other hand, based
on what I see in these boxes, I simply don't think that is the case.

Perhaps they could have designed a cheaper technology, but I believe
a significant portion of the cost of a 800-series CPU as opposed to
a 700-series is the cost of packaging and cooling technology.

Whether that is a *wise* choice is a matter for open debate.

