plugh@NO.SPAM.PLEASE (Caveman) writes:

>Now, I apologize if you can demonstrate awareness of the above issues
>and you still maintain that a K580 or newer server CPU should cost
>less than $1000 to fabricate the package.  On the other hand, based
>on what I see in these boxes, I simply don't think that is the case.

>Perhaps they could have designed a cheaper technology, but I believe
>a significant portion of the cost of a 800-series CPU as opposed to
>a 700-series is the cost of packaging and cooling technology.


I'm not talking about the K580 stuff, those PA-8200 CPU + cache arrays
had tremendous heat loads.  Not only that, but the design of the K was
such that the CPU was on a giant custom card.  The PA-8[56]00 is a far
more simple design since there is no external cache to add to the heat
load, and it is in .25u technology rather than .5u.  IIRC the guesses
I've seen for the PA-8500 wattage (since HP never announced it) ranged
around 70-80 watts.  That's not much more than your typical PC processor
around the time Intel's process runs out of gas (anyone know the figures
offhand for the 600MHz Pentium III in .25u?)  I recall something about
the .35u 21264 running well in excess of 100 watts, not including the
cache.  I'm sure even the .18u Alphas won't be shy about their power
needs once they start clocking >1GHz (not to mention the Pentium IIIs
and Athlons, which will beat Alpha to that mark this month....if you
told me that five years ago, I would have thought you were insane!)

As you noted, the HP stuff isn't exactly high volume -- nothing in the
RISC Unix world, by comparison to x86 or even Apple.  So it is going to
cost more based on that, since even if you can take the overclocker
freak giant heatsinks and adequately cool a PA-8500 (and I'm sure you
can) the reliability and tolerances a Unix server vendor requires won't
be there.  So the parts must be custom, and will be more expensive due
to that.  But it isn't because cooling the PA-8500 is a hard problem,
because it certainly is not.  HP left the "hard cooling problems" behind
when they introduced the PA-8500 and beyond (some will argue that Merced
will bring them back, but at least they'll have experience :) )

