link to http://www.pc-disk.de/pcdisk/.  This provided the answer. It has
a page where the specs for many drives are available. Unfortunately,
this great service may be slow to access since the spec sheets are
available as GIF files instead of plain HTML. But there is an ASCII
version that is OK, but my browser version cannot see properly. (All
these upper case A's with umlauts show up instead of _ . Could it be the
fact that the page was created in Germany? Or that the so-called
ISO-8859-1 character set is not tru ASCII? Oh, well, another mystery for
me.)

So, if you are out there looking for disk specifications, jumper
settings, installation instructions, layout, etc., for hard drives, this
is one place to look.


