<38BE7139.28457844@netinsight.se>...
>Hans Vlems wrote:
>>
>> If the question was: "what is a TU81" then this is it:
>>
>> A 9 track reel tapedrive. It can handle 1600 and 6250 bps densities.
>> It was available with a BI interface (TU81) and a STI interface (TA81).
>
>Could someone expand on these interfaces?
>I know of SDI. STI cables obviously are interchangable. Are there any
>electrical differences?

Nope, SDI and STI are the same interface electrically.
The device that lives at the back end just happens to differ, that's all.

>
>Protocol-wise we also have MSCP for disks, and TMSCP for tapes, but
>that don't show at this level, I believe. Both the TU81 and TA81 talked
>TMSCP.


Hmmm, that's not quite true. Neither unit new about (T)MSCP.
It is the host it attaches to that handles MSCP. For a TUnn tape or a RAnn
disk
that was a VMS host. The TAnn tapedrives and the RAnn disks connected to
things called hierarchical storage controllers (e.g. HSC50) and the HSC's
talked to the VMS (or TOPS) hosts. Yes, two operating systems, one ran on a
36 bit computer called a PDP-10 (and other names, IMHO less polite).

>Or is that totally in the controller, and then you have SDI, STI and BI
>as a lower level communication protocol?


BI (Bus Interconnect) is a system bus in a VAX or an Alpha. SDI and STI
are device interface specifications. For the SDI interface for instance you
have:

KDM70    XMI bus    VAX 6000's    talks both SDI and STI
KDB50    BI                VAX8000's    SDI only
KDA50    Qbus          VAX 3400      SDI only

>And while I'm at it. Why did they do the TU81, with that interface?
>Where was the need? I mean, for disks, you only used SDI, unless
>you count the R80 disk, which also came with massbus, but that
>meant you didn't even treat the disk as an MSCP device.


The R80 was not Massbus I think. The RM80 atteched to Massbus.
The R80 was something which talked to a special controller, together
with a another disk device, on an 11/725 or so. I have no personal
experiences
with these devices. The R(whatever)80 was a nice disk in its day, but the
other disk had one fixed spindle and one removable volume both driven
by the same motor and logic.  I forgot the name (RD25 ?)


