  TCP/IP Services currently (and has traditionally) timed-out a
  sufficiently inactive FTP server process...  

  From one of the engineers:

    "Historically, the INETACP has deleted the FTP server process
    after 15 minutes of inactivity (control & data). I believe that
    one could use the $TCPIP set service/inactivity command to
    vary the default time.

    In the days when processes were expensive, deleting an inactive 
    process made sense. Now, keeping a process around after it has 
    been created is cheap. The expensive part is creating it in
    the first place."

  TCP/IP Engineering is seriously considering kernel changes that
  will (as a side-effect) eliminate this particular timeout.

  Questions?  Comments?  Feedback?  Do you use/depend on this?
 

