Your observation that our discussion went slowly off-topic is right.
And I'm afraid that I wasn't too clear in my 2nd post either.

SDI/STI is a protocol between a controller and a peripheral (disk, resp.
tape).
MSCP is a protocol which allows HSC's (HSJ's and others) to make an SDI/STI
peripheral available to cluster members. It was later implemented on VMS as
well.

So indeed the SDI protocol differs from the MSCP protocol.

Back to the TU81 which started all this: the TU81 and the TA81 share the
same
drive hardware, only the interfaces differ. A TU81 talks to a controller
which sits
on a system bus (examples: BI, Qbus). The TA81 talks STI and connects to
HSCxx devices, or a KDM70 which is the only controller which supports STI
and SDI at the same time on a system bus. The others only do SDI.

You stated that the original poster was incorrect when he wrote that his
TU81 had
a BI interface while you have one on a PDP-11. The point I tried to make in
the
1st post was that the TU81 was sold with various controllers, each for its
own
bus: Unibus, Qbus and BI. Again, none of these talk STI.
There was a time when Pertec more or less dictated tape protocols and it
could
be that the TU81 talked Pertecese...

"VMS talks (T)MSCP to the controller": VMS (and HSXnn's) talk MSCP to each
other.
The peripheral on the back end has no idea it is served on whatever bus (CI,
NI, Fiber Channel)
to other cluster members.

Hans Vlems

