Johnny Billquist <Johnny.Billquist@netinsight.se> writes:
> But the original poster claimed that the TU81 had a BI-interface.
> Was he talking about the interface of the controller to the
> tape then? If so, it's incorrect. I have a TU81 on a PDP-11 here,
> which means I should say that the TU81 has a Unibus interface.

  No, the TU81 has a LESI (Low End Storage Interconnect), a single 50-pin
interface. The controller for that was a KLESI. They were available for
both the Unibus and Q-bus, and presumably for the BI bus as well.

  The KLESI is pretty much just a bus adapter, since the TU81 has a "TMSCP
board" in it (which is what the KLESI bus attaches to).

  If you strip out the TMSCP board, you have a drive with a formatted Per-
tec interface which you can connect just about anywhere else. The drive
was built by Magnetic Peripherals, Inc. which used to be the Magnetic Per-
ipherals Division (MPD) of Control Data Corp. (CDC). Parts of MPI/MPD were
sold to Seagate (at least the disk products, I'm not sure about the tapes)
which is why Seagate disk quality went up some years ago.

  MPI/MPD sold this same drive in both horizontal and vertical mounting
flavors (the drives are actually interchangeable) to many OEM customers.

> Argh! Sorry about that one. I forgot that there actually was something
> called an R80. I used the name R80 to denote the generic HDA. Note that
> I said *also*. The RA80 was SDI (MSCP?), the RM80 was massbus.
> I'm not sure at all whatever the R80 had. Maybe it was the HDA without
> an external interface at all, special hack for the 11/725. Hmmm.
> it should also have been usable in the 11/730 I guess.

  That controller was the RB730. It had a 60-pin and 26-pin connector, just
like the SMD connectors, but I'm not sure if the bus itself was crippled.
The RB730 itself was rather crippled - a lot of the protocol stuff was done
by the driver instead of controller logic, which is why it was a 2-board
set (as I recall) instead of the whole pile-o-boards in the MBA + RH. Of
course, 3rd parties had been building nice single-board SMD controllers for
some time (like the Emulex SCxx products) but then again, DEC stuck w/ TTL
controllers a lot longer than the 3rd parties.

> RC25 I believe. I have experience with both of them. I think I still have
> one RA80 lying somewhere. The RC25 is better left in an unmarked grave.

  RC25. Also an MPI/MPD product, also used the LESI controller. The TK25
was the matching tape drive, which also used a 50-pin cable but wasn't the
same as LESI.

