In article <89jf6t$66k$1@info.service.rug.nl>, helbig@man.ac.uk (Phillip Helbig) writes:
:I have an ALPHAstation 255/233 which has a 2 GB and a 1 GB and a CD-ROM
:inside.  That's all I can put inside.  It is possible to connect more 
:external devices on the same bus.

  Yep, I regularly use a set of RZ2x-VA disks (narrow, obviously) in 
  a BA35x-series box hanging off an AlphaStation 255/300.  Works fine...

:Am I correct in assuming that newer, i.e. fast wide, ultra, whatever 
:SCSI disks will work fine (I have VMS 7.2-1 and the latest firmware 
:update), though of course the speed will be limited by the fast narrow 
:SCSI 2 of the ALPHAstation?

  Yes.  Configuring Ultra widgets can be a little interesting (as both
  fast and Ultra disks tend to have specific thermal requirements, and 
  as Ultra is yet another permutation for proper SCSI negotiation) but 
  it is possible..  Yes, speed will be limited to that of the controller, 
  and the various SCSI permutations are (allegedly) compatible...

  Place a high thermal output disk in an under-rated storage enclosure 
  and you'll cook it -- it'll work fine for a while, but its lifespan 
  will tend to be rather shorter than you might like...    Beware of 
  placing these disks inside under-rated system enclosures, as well...

:Is there any reason to prefer a second SCSI controller for external 
:disks?  If so, what is the price one pays for having all on one 
:controller?

  More bus activity on a shared bus, and more bus length.  The former 
  can lead to bus contention, while the latter can lead to down-selecting 
  SCSI speeds and to the usual raft of flaky over-long SCSI bus errors...
  Depending on the available slots, you can obviously also choose to add
  in a faster controller.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




