Gerald Heinig <Gerald.Heinig@post.rwth-aachen.de> writes:
> I�m a Tru64 (though not Unix) newbie and I have a *really* weird
> problem: a colleague (No, it wasn't me, honest :-) ) did a
> 
> chmod -R 774 *
> 
> on /u01. The filesystem is AdvFS. This chmod also changed *all* the
> permissions on /etc and possibly on / as well!! How the hell can *that*
> happen??

One way it can happen is if /u01 contains a link up to the parent
(root) directory, and you use the chmod from the GNU fileutils
package.  '*' won't include '..', but if you have a soft link to '..'
or '/' with a name that is in the expansion of '*', GNU chmod will
follow it.  I believe there are other ways as well--that's just an
example.  'chmod -R 774 .' is safer.

> Also, users can�t log in anymore getting  "No shell" errors.

Perhaps it did /usr/bin as well, and your users now don't have
permission to execute their shells?  If so, I recommend reading the
man page for fverify, which can at least fixup the permissions of the
operating system supplied files.
